VALUES

-- Q1: hard / L4 — 501(h) expenditure test: evaluating lobbying limit violations
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Riverdale Community Foundation, a 501(c)(3) public charity with $800,000 in exempt purpose expenditures, elects the Section 501(h) expenditure test. The lobbying nontaxable amount is $145,000 and the grassroots nontaxable amount is $36,250. The foundation spends $130,000 on direct lobbying and $40,000 on grassroots lobbying. Evaluate which limits have been exceeded.', '["Both limits are exceeded because total lobbying of $170,000 exceeds $145,000 and grassroots of $40,000 exceeds $36,250, triggering a 25% excise tax under Section 4911", "Only the grassroots limit is exceeded because direct and grassroots lobbying are tested independently against separate caps", "Neither limit is exceeded because the 501(h) election exempts all lobbying below 25% of exempt purpose expenditures", "Only the overall limit is exceeded because grassroots lobbying is included within direct lobbying and has no separate cap"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(h) and Section 4911, the lobbying nontaxable amount covers all lobbying expenditures combined. Total lobbying of $170,000 ($130,000 direct + $40,000 grassroots) exceeds the $145,000 overall limit, and the $40,000 grassroots amount separately exceeds the $36,250 sublimit. A 25% excise tax applies to excess amounts. Unlike Choice B, both types are combined for the overall test; unlike Choice D, grassroots lobbying has its own separate sublimit.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Dissolution clause requirement in governing documents
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What must a 501(c)(3) organization''s governing documents include regarding the distribution of assets upon dissolution?', '["Assets must be distributed equally among the organization''s officers and directors", "Assets must be distributed to another exempt organization or to the government", "Assets must be returned to the original donors in proportion to their contributions", "Assets must be held in escrow for five years before any distribution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the organizational test requires a dissolution clause providing that upon dissolution, remaining assets will be distributed to another 501(c)(3) organization or to a governmental entity. This prevents exempt assets from reverting to private parties. Unlike Choice A, distribution to officers and directors would violate the private inurement prohibition.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Form 990-PF filing requirement for all private foundations
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which annual return must all private foundations file regardless of their financial size or gross receipts?', '["Form 990", "Form 990-EZ", "Form 990-PF", "Form 990-N"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 6033, all private foundations must file Form 990-PF annually regardless of financial size. This detailed return includes excise tax computations under Sections 4940 through 4945. Unlike Form 990 or 990-EZ, which have size thresholds, Form 990-PF applies to every private foundation. Form 990-N is only for small organizations with gross receipts of $50,000 or less and is unavailable to private foundations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Churches as automatic public charities under Section 509(a)(1)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Grace Fellowship Church receives 95% of its financial support from a single wealthy family. A board member questions whether concentrated funding causes the church to be classified as a private foundation. How is Grace Fellowship classified under Section 509?', '["It is a private foundation because it fails the one-third public support test under Section 509(a)(1)", "It is a private foundation but may elect public charity status by filing Form 8940", "Its classification depends on whether it applies for a private foundation exemption within 27 months of formation", "It is a public charity regardless of funding sources because churches are automatically classified as public charities under Section 509(a)(1)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1), churches are automatically classified as public charities regardless of their funding sources. The one-third public support test does not apply to churches, schools, or hospitals. Unlike Choice A, concentrated funding from a single family does not affect a church''s public charity status. This automatic classification reflects Congress''s recognition that churches serve inherently public purposes.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Controlled entity UBIT on rental income exceeding FMV
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Hope Medical Center, a 501(c)(3) hospital, owns 100% of MedSupply Corp, a taxable C corporation. MedSupply pays $200,000 in annual rent to Hope for use of a warehouse. An independent appraisal establishes fair market rent at $120,000. Analyze how this rental income is treated for UBIT purposes under the controlled entity rules.', '["The $80,000 excess over fair market value is included in UBIT because controlled entity payments exceeding FMV are treated as unrelated business income", "The entire $200,000 is included in UBIT because all rental income from a controlled subsidiary is unrelated business income", "No portion is included in UBIT because real property rents are excluded regardless of the payor", "The entire $200,000 is excluded because the rent supports the hospital''s exempt medical purpose"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(13), rent received from a controlled entity (80%+ ownership) is included in UBIT to the extent it exceeds fair market value. The $80,000 excess ($200,000 paid minus $120,000 FMV) is treated as unrelated business income. Unlike Choice B, only the amount exceeding FMV is taxable, not the entire payment. Unlike Choice C, the general real property rent exclusion does not fully apply to above-FMV controlled entity payments.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Debt-financed income ratio calculation under Section 514
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Lighthouse Education Foundation, a 501(c)(3), purchases a commercial building for $1,000,000 using $600,000 in mortgage financing and $400,000 in cash. The building generates $100,000 in annual net rental income. The average acquisition indebtedness is $600,000 and the average adjusted basis is $1,000,000. Evaluate the UBIT treatment under the debt-financed property rules.', '["No rental income is subject to UBIT because real property rents are always excluded under Section 512(b)(3)", "The entire $100,000 is subject to UBIT because the property was acquired with any amount of debt", "$60,000 is subject to UBIT because the debt-financed percentage is 60% ($600,000 divided by $1,000,000) applied to the $100,000 rental income", "$40,000 is subject to UBIT because only the equity-financed portion generates taxable income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 514, income from debt-financed property is subject to UBIT in proportion to the average acquisition indebtedness divided by the average adjusted basis. Here, $600,000/$1,000,000 = 60%, so 60% of the $100,000 rental income ($60,000) is included in UBIT. Unlike Choice A, the real property rent exclusion does not apply to debt-financed property. Unlike Choice B, only the debt-financed percentage is taxable, not the full amount.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Section 4945 expenditure responsibility for grants to non-501(c)(3) organizations
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Morrison Family Foundation, a private foundation, makes a $150,000 grant to Community Builders Inc., a 501(c)(4) social welfare organization, to fund affordable housing. The foundation does not require separate accounts, expenditure reports, or return of unexpended funds. Evaluate the consequences under Section 4945.', '["No penalty applies because grants for affordable housing are automatically treated as charitable expenditures", "The grant is a taxable expenditure only if Community Builders uses the funds for lobbying activities", "The grant triggers a 10% initial excise tax because the foundation failed to verify Community Builders'' exempt status", "The $150,000 is a taxable expenditure subject to a 20% initial excise tax because the foundation failed to exercise expenditure responsibility over a grant to a non-501(c)(3) organization"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 4945(d)(4) and (h), grants by a private foundation to organizations other than 501(c)(3) public charities require expenditure responsibility, including separate accounts, detailed reports, and return of unexpended funds. Failure triggers a 20% initial excise tax on the foundation. Unlike Choice A, the charitable nature of the project does not eliminate the expenditure responsibility requirement. Unlike Choice B, the violation occurs regardless of how funds are used.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Convenience exclusion from UBIT for campus services
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Central University, a 501(c)(3), operates a campus laundry facility available only to enrolled students living in dormitories. The facility charges below-market rates and generates $45,000 in annual revenue. Which UBIT exclusion applies to this income?', '["The volunteer labor exclusion, because student workers operate the facility", "The convenience exclusion, because the activity is conducted primarily for the convenience of students", "The donated goods exclusion, because students donate unclaimed items to the facility", "The passive income exclusion, because laundry service fees are classified as royalties"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 513(a)(2), income from a trade or business carried on primarily for the convenience of members, students, patients, or employees is excluded from UBIT. A campus laundry serving only dormitory students qualifies for this exclusion. Unlike Choice A, the volunteer labor exclusion requires substantially all work to be performed by unpaid volunteers, which is not indicated here. The convenience exclusion protects activities serving the campus community.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Private inurement prohibition definition
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What does the private inurement prohibition under Section 501(c)(3) prevent?', '["Tax-exempt organizations from earning any revenue beyond their operating costs", "Tax-exempt organizations from investing in marketable securities", "Any part of the organization''s net earnings from benefiting private shareholders or individuals who are insiders", "Tax-exempt organizations from paying reasonable salaries to their employees"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the private inurement prohibition prevents any part of a tax-exempt organization''s net earnings from inuring to the benefit of private shareholders or individuals, meaning insiders such as founders, officers, and directors. This does not prohibit paying reasonable compensation. Unlike Choice A, organizations may earn revenue exceeding costs; unlike Choice D, reasonable salaries are permitted and expected.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Personal property rental income exceeding 10% incidental threshold
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Horizon Arts Foundation, a 501(c)(3), leases a furnished studio to a production company. The lease specifies $4,000 monthly for the studio space (real property) and $600 for furniture and equipment (personal property), totaling $4,600. Apply the UBIT modification rules for mixed real and personal property rentals.', '["Only the $600 personal property portion is included in UBIT because it exceeds the 10% incidental threshold under the mixed rental modification rules", "The entire $4,600 is excluded from UBIT because any lease that includes real property qualifies for the full rental exclusion", "The entire $4,600 is included in UBIT because mixing personal property with real property taints the entire lease", "Only $460 is included in UBIT, representing exactly 10% of the total monthly rent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(3), rents from real property are generally excluded from UBIT, but rents from personal property are included unless the personal property component is incidental (less than 10% of total rents). Here, $600/$4,600 = 13%, exceeding the 10% threshold, so the $600 personal property portion is included in UBIT while the $4,000 real property rent remains excluded. Unlike Choice B, the personal property component does not qualify for the incidental exception at 13%.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — One-third public support test under Section 509(a)(1)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Helping Hands Alliance, a 501(c)(3), receives $400,000 in total support: $150,000 from government grants, $100,000 from small individual donations, and $150,000 from a single large donor. How does this affect its classification under Section 509(a)(1)?', '["It is a private foundation because no single category of support exceeds one-third of total support", "It is a public charity only if it files an annual public support schedule with the IRS", "It is a private foundation because the $150,000 large donation exceeds one-third of total support", "It qualifies as a public charity because combined public and government support ($250,000) exceeds one-third of total support"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and Section 170(b)(1)(A)(vi), an organization qualifies as a public charity if it normally receives a substantial part of its support from the general public or government. Here, $150,000 (government) + $100,000 (public donations) = $250,000, which is 62.5% of total support, exceeding one-third. Unlike Choice C, a single large donation does not disqualify the organization if overall public support is sufficient.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Form 990-N (e-Postcard) gross receipts threshold
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the maximum annual gross receipts threshold for a tax-exempt organization to file Form 990-N (e-Postcard)?', '["$50,000", "$100,000", "$200,000", "$500,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 6033, tax-exempt organizations with annual gross receipts normally $50,000 or less may file Form 990-N (e-Postcard), an electronic notice requiring only basic information such as name, address, and EIN. Organizations exceeding $50,000 must file Form 990-EZ or Form 990. Unlike the $200,000 threshold in Choice C, which triggers the full Form 990, the e-Postcard is limited to the smallest organizations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Bingo game statutory exclusion from UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Maple Valley Veterans Association, a 501(c)(3), holds weekly bingo nights generating $35,000 annually. No commercial bingo parlors operate within the jurisdiction. The bingo games meet the three-part UBIT test. Is this bingo income subject to UBIT?', '["Yes, because bingo income always meets the regularly carried on prong of the UBIT test", "No, because legal bingo games are excluded from UBIT when commercial bingo is not ordinarily carried on in the jurisdiction", "No, because all gaming activities are automatically exempt for veterans organizations", "Yes, because weekly frequency makes it a commercial activity subject to corporate tax rates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 513(f), bingo games are excluded from UBIT if the game is legal under state and local law and commercial bingo is not regularly carried on in the same jurisdiction. No commercial bingo parlors operate in the area, satisfying the exclusion. Unlike Choice A, meeting the three-part test does not override a specific statutory exclusion. Unlike Choice C, there is no blanket gaming exemption for veterans organizations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — University research exclusion requiring public availability
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'State University, a 501(c)(3), conducts pharmaceutical research under a $500,000 contract with a private manufacturer. The research results are proprietary and available only to the manufacturer. Does this income qualify for the UBIT research exclusion?', '["Yes, because all research conducted by a university is automatically excluded from UBIT", "Yes, because the research furthers the university''s educational mission", "No, because the research exclusion for universities requires that results be freely available to the general public", "No, because pharmaceutical research is classified as a commercial activity regardless of who performs it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(8) and (9), research conducted by a college or university is excluded from UBIT only if the results are freely available to the general public. Proprietary research for a private manufacturer does not qualify. Unlike Choice A, the university research exclusion is not automatic and depends on public availability of results. Unlike Choice D, the type of research is not determinative; public availability is the key factor.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Section 4941 additional 200% tax for failure to correct self-dealing
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'A private foundation''s founder leases personal office space to the foundation at below-market rent. The IRS identifies the lease as self-dealing under Section 4941 and imposes the initial 10% excise tax. The founder fails to unwind the lease during the correction period. What additional consequence applies?', '["The foundation loses its tax-exempt status immediately upon the IRS determination", "The IRS imposes an additional 10% excise tax, doubling the total penalty to 20%", "The IRS revokes the founder''s ability to serve on the board of any private foundation", "An additional 200% excise tax is imposed on the founder for failure to correct the self-dealing transaction within the taxable period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 4941(b), if a self-dealing transaction is not corrected within the taxable period, an additional excise tax of 200% of the amount involved is imposed on the self-dealer. The initial 10% tax under Section 4941(a) applies immediately, but the 200% additional tax incentivizes prompt correction. Unlike Choice A, exempt status is not automatically revoked for self-dealing; unlike Choice B, the additional tax is 200%, not merely another 10%.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Supporting organization classification under Section 509(a)(3)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Heritage Education Trust is organized and operated exclusively to raise funds for Oakdale Public Schools, a 501(c)(3) public charity. Heritage distributes all net income to Oakdale and conducts no independent programs. How is Heritage classified under Section 509?', '["As a private foundation because it does not directly carry out educational activities", "As a private operating foundation because it distributes income to another exempt organization", "As a public charity under Section 509(a)(1) because it supports an educational institution", "As a public charity under Section 509(a)(3) because it is a supporting organization operated exclusively to benefit Oakdale Public Schools"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(3), an organization qualifies as a public charity if it is organized and operated exclusively for the benefit of one or more specified public charities. Heritage meets this test by existing solely to support Oakdale. Unlike Choice A, supporting organizations are not treated as private foundations despite lacking independent programs. Unlike Choice C, Section 509(a)(1) applies to organizations receiving broad public support, not supporting organizations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — Form 990 filing deadline for calendar-year organizations
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Bright Future Foundation, a 501(c)(3) with a calendar tax year ending December 31, needs to determine its Form 990 filing deadline. When is the return due?', '["May 15 of the following year", "March 15 of the following year", "April 15 of the following year", "June 15 of the following year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 6033, Form 990 is due on the 15th day of the 5th month after the end of the organization''s tax year. For a calendar-year organization, this is May 15. Unlike Choice B (March 15, which applies to S corporations and partnerships) or Choice C (April 15, which applies to individual returns), the Form 990 deadline follows the 5th-month rule.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — NOL deduction computed using only unrelated business items
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Lakeside Charity, a 501(c)(3), had $50,000 of unrelated business taxable income in 2025 and a $30,000 net operating loss from unrelated business activities in 2024. It also had a $20,000 loss from exempt-purpose programs in 2024. Apply the UBIT NOL rules to determine 2025 unrelated business taxable income.', '["$0, because both the $30,000 UBI loss and the $20,000 exempt-activity loss offset the $50,000 income", "$20,000, because only the $30,000 NOL from unrelated business activities may be carried forward against UBIT", "$50,000, because net operating loss carryforwards are not permitted for unrelated business income", "$30,000, because the $20,000 exempt-activity loss offsets UBIT before the NOL deduction applies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(6), net operating losses for UBIT purposes are computed using only unrelated business income items. The $30,000 UBI loss from 2024 carries forward, reducing 2025 UBIT to $20,000 ($50,000 minus $30,000). Losses from exempt-purpose programs cannot offset UBIT. Unlike Choice A, the $20,000 exempt-activity loss is excluded from the UBIT NOL computation. Unlike Choice C, NOL carryforwards are specifically permitted for UBIT.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Tax rate applied to UBIT for exempt corporations
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'At what rate is unrelated business taxable income taxed for a 501(c)(3) organization organized as a corporation?', '["A flat 15% rate", "A 10% excise tax rate", "Regular corporate income tax rates", "Capital gains tax rates"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 511, tax-exempt organizations that are corporations pay UBIT at regular corporate income tax rates (currently 21% under Section 11). Tax-exempt trusts pay at trust income tax rates. Unlike Choice A, there is no special reduced rate for UBIT. The purpose of UBIT is to eliminate the competitive advantage exempt organizations would otherwise have over taxable businesses conducting similar activities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Form 990 Schedule A purpose
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which Form 990 schedule reports a tax-exempt organization''s public charity status and public support calculations?', '["Schedule B", "Schedule D", "Schedule J", "Schedule A"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Schedule A of Form 990 reports the organization''s public charity classification and public support test calculations under Sections 509(a)(1) and 509(a)(2). Schedule B reports contributors (confidential), Schedule D reports supplemental financial statements, and Schedule J reports compensation information for officers and key employees. Understanding each schedule''s purpose is essential for Form 990 compliance.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Late filing penalty for Form 990
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Neighborhood Arts Council, a 501(c)(3) with annual gross receipts of $400,000, files its Form 990 sixty days late without reasonable cause. What penalty does the IRS impose?', '["$20 per day for each day the return is late, up to the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts", "$100 per day with no maximum cap", "A flat penalty of $500 regardless of how late the return is filed", "No penalty because tax-exempt organizations are exempt from late filing penalties"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 6652(c)(1)(A), organizations with gross receipts under $1,186,000 face a penalty of $20 per day the return is late, up to the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts. For $400,000 in gross receipts, the cap is $10,500. Unlike Choice B, the $100/day rate applies only to larger organizations with gross receipts of $1,186,000 or more. Unlike Choice D, exempt organizations are subject to filing penalties.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Form 8868 purpose
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the purpose of IRS Form 8868 for a tax-exempt organization?', '["To apply for initial tax-exempt status under Section 501(c)(3)", "To request an automatic extension of time to file Form 990", "To report unrelated business income and compute UBIT", "To elect the Section 501(h) expenditure test for lobbying"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Form 8868 is used by tax-exempt organizations to request an automatic extension of up to six months to file their annual information return (Form 990, 990-EZ, 990-PF, or 990-T). Unlike Choice A, Form 1023 or 1023-EZ is used to apply for 501(c)(3) status. Unlike Choice C, Form 990-T is used to report UBIT. The extension provides additional filing time but does not extend the time to pay any tax owed.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: hard / L3 — Section 4958 additional 200% tax for failure to correct excess benefit
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'The executive director of Community Outreach Network, a 501(c)(3), receives $750,000 in total compensation when comparable executives earn $250,000. The IRS determines the excess benefit is $500,000 and imposes the initial 25% excise tax of $125,000. The executive director does not return the excess compensation within the correction period. Apply the Section 4958 additional tax rules.', '["An additional 100% tax of $500,000 is imposed, bringing total taxes to $625,000", "An additional 25% tax of $125,000 is imposed, doubling the total to $250,000", "An additional 200% tax of $1,000,000 is imposed, bringing total taxes to $1,125,000", "No additional tax applies because the initial 25% is the maximum penalty under Section 4958"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 4958(b), if the excess benefit transaction is not corrected within the taxable period, an additional 200% excise tax is imposed on the disqualified person. Here, 200% of $500,000 = $1,000,000, plus the initial $125,000, totaling $1,125,000. Unlike Choice A, the additional tax is 200%, not 100%. Unlike Choice D, the statute specifically imposes escalating penalties to incentivize prompt correction of excess benefit transactions.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Form 990 Schedule J for compensation disclosure
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Pinnacle Health Alliance, a 501(c)(3), pays its CFO $425,000 in salary plus $75,000 in benefits. The board wants to ensure proper disclosure on its Form 990. On which schedule must this compensation be reported?', '["Schedule A, which reports public charity support calculations", "Schedule J, which reports compensation for officers, directors, and key employees", "Schedule B, which reports the schedule of contributors", "Schedule O, which reports supplemental narrative information"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Form 990 Schedule J requires detailed reporting of compensation for officers, directors, trustees, key employees, and highest compensated employees. The CFO''s total compensation of $500,000 must be disclosed on Schedule J. Unlike Schedule A (public charity status) or Schedule B (contributors), Schedule J is specifically designed to provide transparency about executive compensation at tax-exempt organizations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Gross receipts test under Section 509(a)(2) for public charity status
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Green Valley Community Center, a 501(c)(3) that does not qualify under Section 509(a)(1), receives $300,000 in total support: $80,000 from membership fees, $60,000 from program service revenue, $50,000 from small donations, and $110,000 from a government contract. Apply the Section 509(a)(2) gross receipts test.', '["Green Valley qualifies as a public charity because contributions, membership fees, and gross receipts from related activities ($190,000) exceed one-third of total support", "Green Valley is a private foundation because program service revenue cannot count toward the one-third support test", "Green Valley qualifies only if the government contract is classified as a grant rather than a fee-for-service arrangement", "Green Valley is a private foundation because membership fees are excluded from the gross receipts calculation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(2), an organization qualifies as a public charity if it normally receives more than one-third of support from contributions, membership fees, and gross receipts from related activities. Here, $50,000 (donations) + $80,000 (membership fees) + $60,000 (program revenue) = $190,000, or 63% of total support, exceeding one-third. Unlike Choice B, program service revenue is specifically included. Unlike Choice D, membership fees are expressly counted.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Section 4943 combined holdings with disqualified persons
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Parker Family Foundation, a private foundation, owns 12% of the voting stock of TechCo Corp. The foundation''s creator personally owns an additional 15%. Under Section 4943, the permitted combined holding for the foundation and disqualified persons is 20%. Determine whether excess business holdings exist.', '["No excess exists because the foundation''s 12% holding alone is below the 20% limit", "The combined 27% holding exceeds the 20% limit by 7%, requiring the foundation to divest to avoid the 10% excise tax on excess holdings", "The combined holdings are permitted because the 20% limit applies only to the foundation''s direct ownership", "The excess is only 2%, calculated as the foundation''s 12% minus a 10% safe harbor for private foundations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 4943, combined holdings of a private foundation and all disqualified persons in any business enterprise cannot exceed 20% of voting stock. Parker Foundation (12%) plus the founder (15%) = 27%, exceeding the limit by 7%. A 10% initial excise tax applies to excess holdings. Unlike Choice A, the test combines the foundation''s holdings with those of all disqualified persons. Unlike Choice C, both direct and attributed holdings count toward the 20% limit.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Section 4945 taxable expenditure categories
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which of the following is classified as a taxable expenditure by a private foundation under Section 4945?', '["A grant to a public charity for hurricane disaster relief", "Payment of reasonable compensation to the foundation''s executive director", "A grant to a non-501(c)(3) organization without exercising expenditure responsibility", "An investment in U.S. Treasury bonds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 4945(d), taxable expenditures include grants to organizations other than public charities when the foundation fails to exercise expenditure responsibility. Other categories include lobbying, political activity, grants to individuals without IRS-approved procedures, and non-charitable expenditures. Unlike Choice A, grants to public charities are not taxable expenditures. Unlike Choice B, reasonable compensation is a legitimate operating expense.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Regularly carried on prong analysis for seasonal activity
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Westfield University, a 501(c)(3), operates food and beverage concessions during its six home football games each season, earning $90,000 annually. A tax advisor evaluates whether this income is subject to UBIT. Apply the three-part test, focusing on the "regularly carried on" requirement.', '["The income is subject to UBIT because selling food is a trade or business unrelated to education", "The income is exempt only if the concessions are staffed entirely by unpaid volunteers", "The income is subject to UBIT but qualifies for the $1,000 specific deduction, reducing taxable income to $89,000", "The income is not subject to UBIT because operating concessions at six games per year lacks the frequency and continuity to be regularly carried on compared to a year-round commercial food operation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 512(a), an activity must be "regularly carried on" to be subject to UBIT, meaning conducted with frequency and continuity comparable to commercial enterprises. Operating concessions at only six football games lacks the year-round frequency of commercial food service. Unlike Choice A, failing the regularly carried on prong means the three-part test is not fully satisfied regardless of other prongs. Unlike Choice B, the volunteer exclusion is a separate analysis.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Private benefit doctrine vs private inurement distinction
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Community Development Alliance, a 501(c)(3), hires an outside marketing consultant with no connection to any officer or director. The contract pays $300,000 for services with an $80,000 fair market value. Distinguish between the private benefit and private inurement doctrines as they apply to this transaction.', '["The transaction violates the private benefit doctrine because it provides excessive benefit to an outside party, even though the consultant is not an insider subject to the private inurement prohibition", "The transaction violates the private inurement prohibition because any payment exceeding fair market value constitutes inurement regardless of the recipient", "Neither doctrine applies because the consultant is an independent contractor, not an employee or officer of the organization", "Both doctrines apply equally because private benefit and private inurement are interchangeable terms under Section 501(c)(3)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the private benefit doctrine is broader than the private inurement prohibition. Private inurement applies only to insiders (officers, directors, those with substantial influence), while private benefit applies to any person. The consultant is not an insider, so inurement does not apply, but paying $300,000 for $80,000 of services is impermissible private benefit. Unlike Choice D, the two doctrines have distinct scopes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluating public charity status under Section 509(a)(1) with multiple support sources
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Sunrise Literacy Project, a 501(c)(3), has five-year support totaling $500,000: $200,000 from government grants, $100,000 from small-donor fundraising, $180,000 from a single corporate sponsor, and $20,000 from investment income. It is not a church, school, hospital, or supporting organization. Evaluate whether Sunrise qualifies as a public charity under Section 509(a)(1).', '["Sunrise is a private foundation because the $180,000 corporate gift is entirely excluded from the public support numerator", "Sunrise is a public charity because total support exceeding $500,000 automatically qualifies any organization regardless of source concentration", "Sunrise qualifies as a public charity because government grants plus public donations ($300,000) represent 60% of total support, exceeding the one-third threshold", "Sunrise is a private foundation because investment income of $20,000 taints the entire public support calculation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and the regulations, the one-third public support test includes government grants and general public contributions. Government grants ($200,000) plus public fundraising ($100,000) = $300,000, or 60% of total support, clearly exceeding one-third. While large single-donor contributions may face limitations in the detailed calculation, the organization''s broad government and public support is sufficient. Unlike Choice A, government grants alone (40%) already exceed the one-third threshold.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
